WEBVTT

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:04.900
A saved query has a name, and the name is all a caller needs.

00:00:04.900 --> 00:00:09.091
In this video, you run the same query from a Liquid template, from

00:00:09.091 --> 00:00:13.980
a Razor view, and through the web API, with its parameters and its permission.

00:00:18.680 --> 00:00:22.836
Let's open Search, Queries, All Queries in the admin menu.

00:00:22.836 --> 00:00:28.485
It lists the saved queries, and the Blog recipe saves one, named RecentBlogPosts.

00:00:28.485 --> 00:00:33.420
That name is what a template, a view, or an API caller uses to run it.

00:00:33.872 --> 00:00:35.163
Open the query.

00:00:35.163 --> 00:00:39.838
Its SQL selects the document ids of blog posts, most recent first, and

00:00:39.838 --> 00:00:43.775
ends with a parameter named limit, whose default is three.

00:00:43.775 --> 00:00:48.512
Return Documents is checked, so the query returns the posts themselves.

00:00:52.712 --> 00:00:57.114
In Liquid, the Queries property returns a saved query by name, and the

00:00:57.114 --> 00:00:58.679
query filter runs it.

00:00:58.679 --> 00:01:00.764
The result is a list of items.

00:01:00.764 --> 00:01:04.762
The Blog theme loops over them in its template for a blog post.

00:01:06.380 --> 00:01:09.906
On the front end, a blog post page lists the titles of the

00:01:09.906 --> 00:01:12.385
three most recent posts below the post.

00:01:12.385 --> 00:01:15.910
That is the loop at work, with the default limit of three.

00:01:17.510 --> 00:01:21.221
Named arguments of the filter are the parameters of the query.

00:01:21.221 --> 00:01:24.223
Here, limit is five instead of the default three.

00:01:24.223 --> 00:01:26.460
Parameter names are case-sensitive.

00:01:27.210 --> 00:01:31.616
A query that returns content items gives you content items, so you can

00:01:31.616 --> 00:01:35.789
use their properties and filters, such as the display text filter.

00:01:35.789 --> 00:01:40.890
A query that returns rows gives items whose properties are the columns of the row.

00:01:41.640 --> 00:01:45.361
If no query has that name, the property is nil, and so is

00:01:45.361 --> 00:01:47.192
the result of the filter.

00:01:47.192 --> 00:01:52.212
Once the Queries feature is enabled, the Queries property is available in every

00:01:52.212 --> 00:01:54.220
Liquid template of the site.

00:01:58.420 --> 00:02:02.770
In a Razor view, the Orchard helper provides ContentQueryAsync.

00:02:02.770 --> 00:02:06.741
Give it the name of a query and it returns content items.

00:02:06.741 --> 00:02:11.280
DisplayAsync renders each one, here with the Summary display type.

00:02:12.030 --> 00:02:17.145
A second overload takes a dictionary of parameters, the same values you pass

00:02:17.145 --> 00:02:18.829
to the Liquid filter.

00:02:18.829 --> 00:02:20.450
Here, limit is five.

00:02:21.200 --> 00:02:26.985
ContentQueryAsync keeps only the results that have a content item ID and skips the others.

00:02:26.985 --> 00:02:32.167
QueryAsync returns the items as they come, which suits queries that return rows,

00:02:32.167 --> 00:02:35.360
and it returns null when no query has the name.

00:02:36.110 --> 00:02:40.570
Views that use the Orchard Core Razor base class have the Orchard property.

00:02:40.570 --> 00:02:43.157
In any other view, inject IOrchardHelper:

00:02:43.157 --> 00:02:46.020
the query methods are extension methods of it.

00:02:50.220 --> 00:02:54.601
The Queries API is at api, queries, followed by the name of the

00:02:54.601 --> 00:02:57.205
query, and it accepts GET and POST.

00:02:57.205 --> 00:03:02.094
Parameters are a JSON object, sent in the parameters value of the query

00:03:02.094 --> 00:03:04.570
string, or as the body of a POST.

00:03:05.320 --> 00:03:09.720
Parameters from a caller reach the database as SQL parameters, so they are

00:03:09.720 --> 00:03:11.590
safe to take from a request.

00:03:11.590 --> 00:03:15.550
Output expressions in the query text are not, which is why the SQL

00:03:15.550 --> 00:03:17.200
editor warns about them.

00:03:17.950 --> 00:03:20.093
A caller needs a permission:

00:03:20.093 --> 00:03:24.946
Execute Api - All queries for every query, or one permission per query,

00:03:24.946 --> 00:03:27.719
such as Execute Api - RecentBlogPosts.

00:03:27.719 --> 00:03:29.925
Manage queries includes both.

00:03:29.925 --> 00:03:33.770
Without the permission, the endpoint answers Not Found.

00:03:34.520 --> 00:03:39.283
The query endpoint reads a bearer token, not the sign-in cookie of the admin.

00:03:39.283 --> 00:03:44.620
A request without a token is anonymous, so the permissions of the Anonymous role apply.

00:03:44.620 --> 00:03:47.260
A separate video covers getting a token.

00:03:48.918 --> 00:03:53.735
To let anonymous callers run this query, let's edit the Anonymous role on

00:03:53.735 --> 00:03:56.358
the Roles page, under Access Control.

00:03:56.823 --> 00:04:03.480
Search the permissions for RecentBlogPosts, allow Execute Api - RecentBlogPosts, and save.

00:04:03.480 --> 00:04:07.433
Requests with no token can then run this one query.

00:04:09.057 --> 00:04:14.485
A GET request to the RecentBlogPosts address, with no token, returns JSON.

00:04:14.485 --> 00:04:18.827
The items property holds the three most recent blog posts.

00:04:19.281 --> 00:04:23.964
With the parameters value set to a JSON object that sets limit to

00:04:23.964 --> 00:04:26.998
two, the same address returns two posts.

00:04:26.998 --> 00:04:29.241
The query text is unchanged.

00:04:30.841 --> 00:04:34.283
A SQL query returns an object holding its items.

00:04:34.283 --> 00:04:38.044
Lucene and Elasticsearch queries also return a count.

00:04:38.044 --> 00:04:43.271
The items are content items when the query returns them, and rows otherwise.

00:04:47.471 --> 00:04:52.554
Use Liquid in theme templates, Razor in views and code that needs content

00:04:52.554 --> 00:04:55.901
items, and the web API for other applications.

00:04:55.901 --> 00:05:01.371
All three run the same saved query by name, so one definition serves all three.

00:05:03.371 --> 00:05:07.461
A saved query is defined once and run by its name, from a

00:05:07.461 --> 00:05:09.863
template, a view, or a request.

00:05:09.863 --> 00:05:11.551
Thanks for watching.

